<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A circle has centre <m>O</m> and radius <m>{13}</m> cm.</p><p>(a) Chords of length <m>{10}</m> cm are drawn in the circle. Find the radius of the locus of their midpoints, correct to 1 decimal place where it is not a whole number.</p><div><fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/> cm  </div><p>(b) For each chord length, choose the locus of the midpoints of all chords of that length.</p><ol><li><div>Chords of length <m>{28}</m> cm [ a circle with centre O | the single point O | there are no such chords ]  </div></li><li><div>Chords of length <m>{26}</m> cm [ a circle with centre O | the single point O | there are no such chords ]  </div></li><li><div>Chords of length <m>{20}</m> cm [ a circle with centre O | the single point O | there are no such chords ]  </div></li></ol></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) The perpendicular from <m>O</m> to a chord bisects it. If <m>M</m> is the midpoint of a chord <m>AB</m> of length <m>{10}</m> cm, then <m>AM = {5}</m> cm and triangle <m>OMA</m> has a right angle at <m>M</m>:</p><p><me>OM^2 = OA^2 - AM^2 = {13}^2 - {5}^2 = {144}</me></p><p>So <m>OM = {12}</m> cm for every such chord: the midpoints lie on the circle with centre <m>O</m> and that radius.</p><p>(b)</p><ul><li><p>No chord can be longer than the diameter, <m>{26}</m> cm, so there are no chords of <m>{28}</m> cm.</p></li><li><p>A chord of <m>{26}</m> cm is a diameter, so every one has its midpoint at <m>O</m>.</p></li><li><p>A chord of <m>{20}</m> cm is shorter than the diameter, <m>{26}</m> cm, so such chords exist and none passes through O: their midpoints are all <m>\sqrt{ {13}^2 - {10}^2 }</m> cm from <m>O</m>, on a circle with centre <m>O</m>.</p></li></ul></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>12.0</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p>there are no such chords</p></answer>
    <answer><p>the single point O</p></answer>
    <answer><p>a circle with centre O</p></answer>
</stack-static>